# Deny by default, then allow back exactly what the image needs.
#
# The old allow-by-default list kept losing this game. `COPY . .` sweeps in
# whatever happens to be sitting in a developer's working tree, and the
# dangerous files are precisely the ones git already hides -- so they never
# show up in a diff, a review, or `git status`. Real examples caught here:
#
#   backend/storage/          524 files, 38MB of REAL USER chat uploads
#   frontend/voice/assets/    untracked build output holding a Google API key
#   backend/.env.bak-*        a backup of the secrets file
#   langchain_runner/.env     where the workflow editor writes provider keys
#
# Each was gitignored (correctly kept off GitHub) and each would have been
# baked into the image and served over HTTP. Enumerating offenders one by one
# only fixes the ones someone thought of; denying everything and naming what
# the app actually needs fails closed instead.
#
# Rule of thumb when editing: the image should contain what a FRESH CLONE
# contains, plus what the build produces (vendor/). Nothing else.
*

# --- the application itself -------------------------------------------------
!backend
!frontend
!docker
!index.html
!.htaccess
!README.md
!LICENSE

# --- carve back out of the above -------------------------------------------
# Secrets, runtime data and build artefacts that live inside those trees.
#
# `**/vendor`, not `backend/vendor`: backend/tests/, backend/database/,
# backend/migrations/ and backend/examples/ each carry their own full PHPUnit
# install, and naming only the top-level one silently added ~140MB back.
# vendor/ is excluded from the context on purpose -- the Dockerfile runs
# composer install at build time, so the image never depends on the host
# having done so.
#
# Note there is deliberately no `!**/.env.example` here. Docker recreates a
# parent directory in order to hold a re-included child, so that one negation
# resurrected backend_python/ and backend_typescript/ (each just to carry its
# own .env.example) after `*` had correctly excluded them. It is not needed
# anyway: `.env.example` does not match the `.env` patterns below, so the
# copies the entrypoint reads come through with their parent trees.
**/vendor
**/node_modules
**/__pycache__
**/.venv
**/*.log
**/logs
**/.DS_Store
**/.env
backend/storage
backend/.env.bak*
backend/.phpunit.result.cache
frontend/assets/js/config.js
frontend/voice/config.json
frontend/voice/assets

# Note on what is NOT allowed back in: backend_typescript/, backend_python/
# and langchain_runner/ are absent on purpose. The web image is the PHP
# backend; the optional node/python compose profiles BIND-MOUNT their source
# at run time rather than baking it in, and the local runner is installed on
# the host by the setup wizard. Leaving them out keeps a developer's
# langchain_runner/.env out of the image by construction rather than by a
# pattern someone has to remember to maintain.
